{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman from the Crusader states in the Levant He was born around 1140 and died in 1187 He was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem\n\nThe County of Tripoli was one of the Crusader states established in the Levant after the First Crusade It was a vassal state of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was located on the Mediterranean coast of modern-day Lebanon\n\nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was around 12 years old He was still a minor at the time and was placed under the regency of his mother Hodierna and his uncle, the Count of Jerusalem\n\nIn 1157, Raymond III came of age and began to rule the County of Tripoli in his own right He was an active and capable ruler who worked to maintain the security and stability of his county in the face of threats from the Muslim states surrounding it\n\nOne of the major challenges that Raymond III faced during his reign was the rise of the Zengid dynasty, a Muslim dynasty that controlled much of the territory to the east of the County of Tripoli The Zengids were led by Nur ad-Din, a powerful and ambitious ruler who sought to expand his territory at the expense of the Crusader states\n\nRaymond III was able to defend the County of Tripoli against the Zengids, but he was unable to prevent them from conquering the County of Edessa, another Crusader state to the north of Tripoli In 1174, Nur ad-Din died and was succeeded by his son, Saladin, who continued his father's efforts to expand the Zengid territory\n\nSaladin's armies posed a significant threat to the County of Tripoli, and Raymond III sought allies to help him defend his territory He allied himself with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Antioch, and the three states worked together to resist Saladin's advances\n\nIn 1187, Saladin launched a major offensive against the Crusader states He defeated the combined forces of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli at the Battle of Hattin, and as a result, he was able to conquer much of the territory held by the Crusaders in the Levant Raymond III was killed in the battle, and the County of Tripoli was eventually conquered by Saladin in 1188\n\nIn conclusion, Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a capable and active ruler who worked to maintain the security and stability of the County of Tripoli during his reign He faced significant challenges from the Zengid dynasty and Saladin, and he was ultimately unable to prevent the County of Tripoli from being conquered by Saladin's armies.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.3863314986228943}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.1458391547203064}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.07335490733385086}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.001650639227591455}, {"token": "from", "logprob": -1.1010538339614868}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "Cr", "logprob": -0.13685312867164612}, {"token": "us", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.03446865454316139}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.06508392095565796}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -0.0001102625101339072}, {"token": "Lev", "logprob": -0.014092809520661831}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": -7.533743337262422e-05}, {"token": "He", "logprob": -0.02593357302248478}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.0003069168305955827}, {"token": "born", "logprob": -0.07324456423521042}, {"token": "around", "logprob"